@node CMacs AI Brigade
@chapter CMacs AI Brigade
@cindex AI brigade
@cindex agents
@cindex multi-agent orchestration

The brigade is not a feature so much as a @dfn{fabric}: the layer every
other cmacs subsystem---and every user's own code---lays on top of to get
AI capability, agent orchestration, and memory.

It ships out-of-the-box features (a memory index over your notes, an agent
dashboard, GenMail, deliverable generators, AI in imgedit and vidstudio),
but its primary deliverable is the @emph{extension surface}: exposing
something of your own to every agent is one form in your init, and that
one form lights it up on all three delivery paths at once.

@menu
* Brigade Overview::            What the fabric is and why.
* Brigade Requirements::        Configure flags and dependencies.
* Brigade Probing::             Detecting the feature at runtime.
* Brigade Extending::           Registering your own capabilities.
* Brigade Notifications::       Being told when it finishes; voice.
@end menu

@node Brigade Overview
@section Overview

A registered capability becomes callable by:

@enumerate
@item
in-process HTTP agents, as a tool on the agent's executor;
@item
CLI agents (@command{claude-code}, @command{opencode}), as an MCP tool
reached through the @option{--mcp-relay} bridge, scoped by that agent's
capability token;
@item
external MCP clients, on cmacs's existing MCP server.
@end enumerate

Everything the shipped features use goes through the same public API you
get.  There are deliberately no private back doors: if a built-in needs a
hook, that hook is public.

@subsection Design principles

@table @asis
@item Nothing is hardcoded
Every path, model, budget, endpoint and backend is a @code{defcustom}.
Shipped values are @emph{defaults}, never assumptions.  An ERT test scans
the brigade sources and fails if a literal path, model id, or endpoint
appears outside a @code{defcustom} form.

@item Org is the plan, the dashboard is a view
A live Org buffer is the durable plan model, so org-agenda, org-clock,
refile, capture and git history all work on it for free.  C is
authoritative for @emph{runtime}; Org is authoritative for @emph{intent}.
The @code{TODO} keyword is a projection of runtime state and an input
channel for commands, never a stored value.

@item Agents run out of process
A @option{--gowl} cmacs instance @emph{is} the user's Wayland session.  An
agent that blocks or errors in-process takes the desktop with it, so
agents run in supervised children.

@item Capability scoping is real
Each agent gets a randomly minted token and a 0600 @file{.mcp.json} under
@env{XDG_RUNTIME_DIR}; the allowlist is enforced in C at the tool dispatch
point.  An allowlist implemented in Lisp is bypassable by any agent that
reaches @code{eval}, so it is not implemented there.
@end table

@node Brigade Requirements
@section Requirements

The subsystem is gated on @option{--with-cmacs-ai-brigade}, which
@emph{hard-requires} @option{--with-cmacs-ai}; @command{configure} errors
out otherwise, because the brigade drives ai-glib's provider and
tool-executor types directly.

@option{--with-cmacs-libreclaw} is optional and adds extra surfaces when
present.

@node Brigade Probing
@section Probing the build

@defun cmacs-brigade-available-p
Return non-@code{nil} when this cmacs was built with the brigade.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-capabilities
Return a plist of compile-time capabilities: @code{:libreclaw},
@code{:mcp} and @code{:f16c}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-capability key
Return one capability from that plist.  Returns @code{nil} when the
brigade is absent, so callers need not guard first.
@end defun

@code{IS-CMACS-AI-BRIGADE} (and the lower-case alias
@code{is-cmacs-ai-brigade}) is always bound, even in a build with the
feature off, so a user config can branch without a @code{void-variable}
error:

@example
(when IS-CMACS-AI-BRIGADE
  (require 'cmacs-brigade))
@end example

The @code{:f16c} capability reports only that the F16C vector-scan path
could be @emph{compiled}, not that this CPU has the instructions---that is
decided at runtime by @code{__builtin_cpu_supports}, with a scalar
fallback, so a single binary runs on hosts with and without it.

@node Brigade Extending
@section Extending the brigade

@code{cmacs-brigade-deftool} registers a plain Lisp function and publishes
it on all three surfaces:

@example
(cmacs-brigade-deftool weather-now
  "Current conditions for a place, as a short sentence."
  ((place string "City or 'lat,lon'")
   (units string "metric or imperial" :optional t :default "metric"))
  :group 'weather
  (my/fetch-weather place units))
@end example

Tool keywords are @code{:group} (allowlist vocabulary), @code{:destructive}
(excluded from read-only agents), @code{:confirm} (@code{nil}/@code{ask}/
@code{always}), @code{:async} (the body receives a @code{done} callback),
and @code{:timeout}.

The other registries are @code{cmacs-brigade-register-agent},
@code{-worker}, @code{-isolation}, @code{-memory-source},
@code{-deliverable}, @code{-panel}, @code{-context-provider},
@code{-approval-handler} and @code{cmacs-brigade-register-notifier}.

A memory source supplies @code{enumerate} and @code{read-chunk}, so a
Postgres table, a code tree or a Zotero library can be indexed into the
same vector store and every agent's @code{memory_search} covers it.

@subsection Allowlists and the relay

An agent's tool list may name individual tools, groups, or @samp{*}.
Before a CLI agent is spawned that list is expanded by
@code{cmacs-brigade-allowlist-expand}: group names resolve to the tools
in them, while @samp{*} and any unrecognised name pass through.

Expansion happens in cmacs rather than in the relay because the relay
runs in its own process, spawned by the AI CLI, with an empty
registry---a group name reaching that side would match nothing,
silently.  Unrecognised names pass through because most of what an agent
uses (@code{project_read_file}, @code{memory_search}, @code{gowl_*}) is a
built-in MCP tool the brigade registry has never seen.

Two rules the relay enforces with no registry at all:

@itemize @bullet
@item
Tools named @code{ai_*} or @code{brigade_*} are refused
unconditionally, so an agent cannot spawn agents outside the
orchestrator's budget accounting.
@item
The @dfn{privileged set}---@code{eval}, @code{bash}, @code{shell},
@code{execute_command}, @code{send_keys}, the C-patching tools,
@code{crispy_eval}, @code{bacon_eval}---is unreachable through
@samp{*} or a group.  Granting one is a deliberate act: name it
outright or the agent does not get it.
@end itemize

@defun cmacs-brigade-tool-allowed-p allowlist tool-name
Return non-@code{nil} if @var{allowlist} authorises @var{tool-name}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-allowlist-expand allowlist
Resolve group names in @var{allowlist} to concrete tool names.
@end defun

@xref{Top,,, cmacs, the org manual}, file
@file{doc_org/cmacs/ai-brigade/extending.org}, for the full worked
example: a telephony @samp{Call For Me} feature that cmacs deliberately
does not implement, built end to end from a user's own init file.

@node Brigade Notifications
@section Notifications and voice

The expensive failure is not an agent finishing silently.  It is walking
away while one runs and coming back forty minutes after it finished
without knowing.  A notification fired at the moment of completion does
not recover those minutes, because you were not there to hear it.  So
there are three mechanisms:

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Notify when it happens}---speak, play a sound, raise a desktop
notification, run a script, print a receipt, or anything you register.
@item
@strong{Escalate what is blocked.}  A finished agent costs you nothing by
waiting; one waiting on an answer is burning wall-clock doing nothing, so
it repeats at widening intervals until dealt with.
@item
@strong{Report what happened while you were gone.}  Events that fire
while you are away are held and delivered the moment you return---the
first command after a long idle.  This is the mechanism that closes the
gap, because it does not require you to have been present.
@end enumerate

@example
(cmacs-brigade-notify-mode 1)
@end example

@code{cmacs-brigade-notify-routes} maps each event kind
(@code{started}, @code{finished}, @code{failed}, @code{needs-input},
@code{budget-exceeded}, @code{all-done}, @code{away-quiet}) to the
notifiers that fire for it.  The shipped notifiers are @code{message},
@code{speak} (piper), @code{sound}, @code{desktop} (@command{notify-send}),
@code{command} (a script, given the event in its environment) and
@code{receipt}.  A notifier whose @code{:available} predicate returns
@code{nil} is skipped rather than erroring, and one that signals is caught
and logged so the rest of the route still fires.

@defun cmacs-brigade-register-notifier &rest plist
Register a way of getting the user's attention.  Keys: @code{:name},
@code{:notify} (called with the event plist), and optionally
@code{:available}.  The event carries @code{:kind}, @code{:task},
@code{:agent}, @code{:state}, @code{:text} and @code{:urgent}.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-notify-status
Report what the brigade is doing right now, aloud when called
interactively.
@end defun

@defun cmacs-brigade-notify-pending
Return the tasks currently waiting on you.
@end defun

@subsection Voice

@code{cmacs-brigade-voice} adds speech in both directions, needing
whisper for input and piper for output.
@code{cmacs-brigade-voice-listen} is the primitive: it records one phrase
and calls back with the text.  It is deliberately not
@code{cmacs-whisper-dictate}, which is a streaming insert-as-you-talk
toggle---transcribing a phrase whole also transcribes it better, since a
sentence split across three-second windows loses the context that
disambiguates its middle.

@code{cmacs-brigade-voice-task} dictates a task: it is confirmed, then
appended to a plan as an ordinary org headline and adopted, because org
is authoritative for intent and a spoken task should be as editable and
git-tracked as a typed one.  @code{cmacs-brigade-voice-ask} answers four
spoken questions---what is running, what is waiting on me, what has this
cost, what has finished---matched by regexp rather than by a model call,
since the answers are already computed.

@code{cmacs-brigade-voice-setup-approvals} registers an approval handler
that reads a confirmation request aloud.  The answer is still typed:
approval is a synchronous gate, and blocking the editor to record a
spoken yes would stall whatever else is running---under @option{--gowl}
that is the compositor.  It is opt-in rather than automatic because
registering any approval handler replaces the built-in fallback, which
refuses when it cannot ask; a headless session must not have ``could not
ask'' become ``went ahead anyway'' merely because this file was loaded.

In the dashboard the voice commands live under @kbd{v}: @kbd{v t} to
dictate a task, @kbd{v q} to ask, @kbd{v s} to stop recording, @kbd{v ?}
to hear the status.

@xref{Top,,, cmacs, the org manual}, file
@file{doc_org/cmacs/ai-brigade/notifications.org}, for the full chapter.
